hich rows are the symplectic form of the generators of S [18].\n\nTo study what graph states can be reached from a given graph state, we introduce the notion of a qubit-minor as defined in definition 1.1. It turns out that the question of whether |G′〉 is a qubit-minor of |G〉 is equivalent to whether G′ is a vertex-minor of G, as we describe below.\n\n> \n> □\n\nSurprisingly, any single-qubit Clifford operation which takes some graph state to another graph state can be seen as some sequence of local complementations on the corresponding graphs. This was proven in [9] and we restate this theorem here.\n\n> #### Theorem 2.2 ([Van den Nest et al. 9]). —\n> \n> Two graph states |G〉 and |G′〉 are equivalent under single-qubit Clifford operations if and only if their corresponding graphs G and G′ are related by some sequence of local complementations.\n\nLet m=v_{1}v_{2}…v_{l} be a sequence of vertices of G, then we denote the sequence of local complementations on the vertices in m as\n\nImage: (c).  | 2.13\n\nIf there exists a sequence m such that τ_{m}(G)=G′, then we write this as G∼_{LC}G′. Theorem 2.2 can therefore be stated as\n\nImage: (c).  | 2.14\n\nTesting whether two graphs are LC-equivalent can be done in time Image: Inline graphic, where n is the size of the graphs, as shown in [10].\n\n### (d). Local Pauli measurements\n\nHow should the corresponding graph of a graph state be updated when a measurement is performed? In [19], it is shown how the Pauli projectors Image: Inline graphic, Image: Inline graphic, Image: Inline graphic act on graph states^{3}\n\nImage: (d).  | 2.15\nImage: (d).  | 2.16\n | 2.17\n\nwhere e is an edge of G incident on the vertex v. Choosing a different edge e′ incident on v gives a single-qubit Clifford-equivalent graph state, i.e. |T_{e′}(G)∖v〉∼_{LC}|T_{e}(G)∖v〉. The operation T_{(u,v)} is called a pivot and is defined as T_{(u,v)}=τ_{v}°τ_{u}°τ_{v}. The pivot can simply be specified by an undirected edge since\n\nImage: (d).  | 2.18\n\